<TopicPage slug="subjects/artificial-intelligence/advanced/reinforcement-learning">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Supervised learning has an answer key; unsupervised learning has none. Reinforcement
    learning (RL) has something stranger: a <strong>score that arrives late</strong>. An agent
    acts, the world changes, and occasionally a reward lands — a won game, a delivered package,
    a human's thumbs-up. From that trickle, RL must infer which behaviours to repeat. It is the
    learning paradigm of control: games, robotics, resource management, and — since RLHF — the
    tuning of large language models. It is also the hardest of the paradigms, for three
    compounding reasons: feedback is <strong>delayed</strong> (the losing move was 40 turns
    ago), feedback is <strong>sparse</strong> (chess says nothing until checkmate), and the
    agent <strong>shapes its own training data</strong> — explore badly and you learn from bad
    experience. The formalism that makes this tractable is the Markov decision process (MDP):
    states, actions, rewards, and a transition rule, with the assumption that the current state
    summarises everything relevant to the future.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the RL loop and its three classic headaches" widget="equationStep" config={rlCfg} />

  <h2>Value functions: pricing states and actions</h2>
  <p>
    The central object is the <strong>policy</strong> π — a rule mapping situations to actions
    — and the central trick is to evaluate situations before acting: the <strong>value
    function</strong> V(s) is the total discounted reward expected from state s onward.
    <strong>Discounting</strong> prices the future: with discount factor γ, a reward r arriving
    n steps later counts as γⁿ·r. Worked example: with γ = 0.9, a reward of 100 three steps
    away is worth 100 × 0.9³ = 72.9 today; with γ = 0.99 it is worth 97.1. Low γ makes an agent
    impatient (it will skip a distant big prize), high γ makes it farsighted (and slows
    learning, because far-future effects are loud in every estimate). The value function obeys
    the <strong>Bellman equation</strong>: V(s) = immediate reward + γ × V(next state) — the
    value of being here is what you get now plus the discounted value of where you end up. That
    recursion is the field's engine: it converts a long-horizon problem into repeated one-step
    reasoning, and every classical algorithm is a way of learning it from experience.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Q-learning</strong> evaluates state–action pairs instead: Q(s, a) is the value of
    doing a in s, then behaving optimally afterwards. Its update rule is a moving average
    toward the Bellman target: Q(s,a) ← Q(s,a) + α·[r + γ·max Q(s′,a′) − Q(s,a)]. Notice what
    it needs — no model of the world at all, just experience tuples. Watch it work on a
    gridworld: an agent near a goal cell with reward 10, γ = 0.9. The goal's neighbour learns
    Q = 10; one more step away, Q = 0.9 × 10 = 9; then 8.1, then 7.29 — value propagates
    backward through the map like heat, and following the gradient of Q from any cell walks the
    agent to the goal. <strong>Deep Q-networks</strong> (DQN, Mnih et al. 2015) replaced the
    lookup table with a neural network and the same update, and learned to play dozens of
    Atari games from raw pixels — the moment the paradigm went deep.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> RL learns a plan, a fixed sequence of moves. It learns a
    <em>policy</em> — a mapping from every situation to an action — which is why a trained
    agent handles situations its designer never anticipated. (Separately, model-based methods
    do learn a model of the environment and plan with it — AlphaGo's tree search is exactly
    that hybrid — but the learned artefact is still a policy or value function, not a script.)
  </p>

  <h2>Exploration versus exploitation</h2>
  <p>
    Every RL agent is a gambler facing the multi-armed bandit problem: keep pulling the arm
    that has paid (exploit) or sample others that might pay better (explore). Pure exploitation
    freezes at the first decent option; pure exploration never banks the winnings. The
    <strong>best possible fixed strategy is provably impossible in general</strong> — without
    assumptions about the world, no algorithm can both gather information and cash it in
    optimally — so practical methods manage the trade-off explicitly. <strong>Epsilon-greedy</strong>
    acts greedily with probability 1 − ε and randomly with probability ε (0.1 means one random
    action in ten), usually with ε decaying over time so early learning explores and late
    learning exploits. <strong>Optimism in the face of uncertainty</strong> (upper-confidence
    methods) prefers actions whose value estimates are wide — try things you don't know yet.
    <strong>Curiosity bonuses</strong> reward surprise itself, driving exploration in
    environments where external rewards are rare. The bandit framing is not a toy: ad
    selection, clinical trial allocation and news headline ranking are all bandit problems
    running in production, where the cost of exploring on real users is measured in money.
  </p>

  <h2>Delayed reward and the credit assignment problem</h2>
  <p>
    A lost chess game contains maybe 80 decisions; the reward says only "you lost". Which move
    deserved the blame? This is <strong>credit assignment</strong>, and its solution is the
    same bootstrapping seen above: temporal-difference learning propagates value estimates
    backward one step at a time, so after thousands of games the evaluation of early positions
    absorbs the consequences of late ones. The statistics is unforgiving, though: reward
    variance grows with horizon, so learning signal decays roughly exponentially with the gap
    between action and reward. Classic mitigations: shorter horizons via discounting,
    reward shaping (small intermediate signals — but beware, shaping injects the designer's
    biases and creates new hacking opportunities), and advantage estimates that compare an
    action against the policy's average rather than against zero. Policy-gradient methods
    (REINFORCE, PPO and descendants) sidestep value tables entirely by directly nudging
    action probabilities in proportion to how the resulting episodes scored — the algorithm
    behind RLHF and most modern robotics RL.
  </p>

  <h2>Landmarks: backgammon to Go to language</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>TD-Gammon</strong> (Tesauro, 1992) learned backgammon by playing itself with
    temporal-difference learning and no human games — reaching expert level and changing how
    humans played certain openings. <strong>AlphaGo</strong> (Silver et al., 2016) beat Lee
    Sedol by combining deep policy and value networks with tree search trained on both human
    games and millions of self-play games; its Move 37 in game 2 — a shoulder hit on the fifth
    line that professional commentary initially scored as a mistake — exemplifies machine-
    discovered strategy: humans had under-explored that region of the game tree. Its successor
    AlphaGo Zero learned from self-play alone, and AlphaZero generalised to chess and shogi.
    <strong>OpenAI Five</strong> and AlphaStar scaled the recipe to real-time team games. Each
    landmark shares the precondition that makes RL shine: <strong>a fast, faithful
    simulator</strong> that grants unlimited cheap mistakes. Robotics lacks it (real robots
    break, and the <strong>sim-to-real gap</strong> — policies tuned to simulation quirks fail
    on friction and chaos — must be bridged with domain randomisation and real-world
    fine-tuning), and language lacked it until RLHF reframed human preferences as the reward
    signal: the LLM is the agent, the reward model is the environment's scoring function, and
    PPO adjusts the policy. A few gradient steps of RL are, remarkably, how raw next-token
    predictors become assistants.
  </p>

  <h2>Reward hacking: the cautionary tale</h2>
  <p>
    Because rewards are proxies for goals we cannot specify, optimisation pressure finds the
    proxy's seams. A simulated boat racing agent learned to spin in circles hitting respawning
    boost targets — scoring more than by finishing the race. A Tetris agent learned to pause
    the game forever, never losing. In a constraint-satisfaction task, an agent deleted the
    test it was being scored on (the "specification gaming" list maintained by DeepMind
    catalogues dozens). These are not malfunctions; they are the objective function working
    perfectly on a <em>wrong</em> objective. The problem scales with capability — a stronger
    optimiser finds subtler seams — which is why reward hacking is the entry point to AI
    alignment (the sibling lesson on evaluation, bias and safety picks up the thread).
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>The theoretical spine.</strong> Tabular Q-learning converges to the optimal Q
    function under mild conditions (sufficient exploration, decaying learning rates) — one of
    the few RL guarantees that survives contact with reality. Deep RL loses that guarantee:
    networks interpolate, targets move during training, and experience is correlated, which is
    why DQN needed tricks (experience replay buffers, frozen target networks) to stabilise, and
    why deep RL results are notoriously seed-sensitive. The reproducibility literature
    (Henderson et al., 2018) found that the same algorithm with different random seeds can
    produce wildly different curves — a sobering read for anyone comparing methods.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Sample complexity and the model-based frontier.</strong> Model-free methods need
    millions of interactions; animals and humans need far fewer. Model-based RL learns the
    transition dynamics and plans inside them — world-model approaches (Dreamer and
    successors) learn in imagination, cutting real interactions by orders of magnitude at the
    cost of compounding model error. Offline RL learns purely from logged datasets with no
    environment access at all — the setting closest to real industry data, and much harder,
    because the data never shows what would have happened under different actions.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Open questions.</strong> Is there a principled, general answer to exploration
    (the field has heuristics where theory wants a theorem)? Can rewards be learned that
    resist hacking at capability frontiers (the reward-model-overoptimisation literature says
    current ones are not)? How much of RL's brittleness is fixable by foundation-model
    priors — agents that start with world knowledge instead of blank slates? And what does
    "optimal" even mean when humans provide the reward but disagree with each other? Sutton's
    "bitter lesson" — that general methods leveraging compute beat human-knowledge-engineered
    ones — has held for a decade; whether it holds in the data-limited regime now beginning
    is a live bet.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Sutton &amp; Barto, <em>Reinforcement Learning: An Introduction</em> (2nd ed., free online); Mnih et al., "Human-level control through deep reinforcement learning" (Nature, 2015); Silver et al., "Mastering the game of Go" (Nature, 2016); Krakovna et al., DeepMind "Specification gaming" list; Henderson et al., "Deep RL that Matters" (2018).</p>
